WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.195
Here, I have a bunch of different&nbsp;sorting algorithms implemented in C++.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.195 --> 00:00:10.240
We have a quick sort, a bubble&nbsp;sort, a merge sort and an insertion sort.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.864
Let's go ahead and run a benchmark to see which&nbsp;one's actually fastest.

00:00:13.864 --> 00:00:17.143
Here, I'm using the google benchmark library to do the benchmarking.

00:00:17.143 --> 00:00:20.402
Here's&nbsp;the guts of basically what we're timing here.

00:00:20.402 --> 00:00:23.483
Which is that we're going to create a copy of some&nbsp;initial vector.

00:00:23.483 --> 00:00:26.133
And then see how long it takes to sort it.

00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:29.418
Which sorting algorithm do you think is&nbsp;going to bench to pass this?

00:00:29.418 --> 00:00:33.655
Quick sort, Bubble sort, Insertion sort or Merge sort?

00:00:33.655 --> 00:00:35.051
Well, here are the&nbsp;results.

00:00:35.051 --> 00:00:37.913
I did include standard sort here just for comparison sake.

00:00:37.913 --> 00:00:42.471
But of the four that I mentioned&nbsp;before, it looks like insertion sort was the winner.

00:00:44.198 --> 00:00:48.423
In this case, insertion sort and bubble sort&nbsp;performed better than quick sort and merge sort

00:00:48.423 --> 00:00:53.057
which is probably the opposite of what a lot of&nbsp;you might have expected.

00:00:53.057 --> 00:00:56.663
Yes, insertion sort and bubble sort are n-squared algorithms.

00:00:56.663 --> 00:01:01.040
Whereas, quick&nbsp;sort is expected nlogn and merge sort is nlogn.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.697
So, why did the n-squared algorithms beat out the nlogn algorithms in this case?

00:01:05.697 --> 00:01:13.200
Well, of course, it's because I didn't tell you that the size that&nbsp;we're sorting is pretty small, only 16 elements.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.569
But on a fixed small size, like, 16,

00:01:16.569 --> 00:01:20.846
Big-O&nbsp;time complexity of an algorithm is often not the most important factor.

00:01:20.846 --> 00:01:27.020
In fact,&nbsp;cache locality and branch predictability often play a much larger role.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.983
Caches and branch&nbsp;prediction don't have as much to do with C++ itself

00:01:30.983 --> 00:01:34.205
as they have to do with&nbsp;your physical processor and memory.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.000
In a real computer, accessing something off&nbsp;of disk is going to be thousands of times&nbsp;slower

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.800
than accessing from RAM which is&nbsp;going to be slower than accessing from cache.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.840
Of course, the catch is quantity.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.619
You're going to&nbsp;have way less RAM than you do have disk space.

00:01:49.619 --> 00:01:53.513
And likewise, you have way less cache than you have&nbsp;for RAM.

00:01:53.513 --> 00:01:59.197
On my machine, I have 32 gigabytes of RAM but only 8 megabytes of cache.

00:01:59.197 --> 00:02:05.000
Here, I have a struct&nbsp;S that has two members x and y which are going to be stored contiguously in memory.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.684
When I call a&nbsp;function that takes an s, then if I want to access s.x,

00:02:09.684 --> 00:02:11.977
the computer will load that memory

00:02:11.977 --> 00:02:16.269
where&nbsp;this little s is living and put it into cache so it's available.

00:02:16.269 --> 00:02:18.397
But it won't just load in s.x&nbsp;alone.

00:02:18.397 --> 00:02:21.596
It'll actually grab a whole chunk of memory that's nearby.

00:02:21.596 --> 00:02:25.998
So that later, when I very likely&nbsp;want to access other attributes, like, s.y,

00:02:25.998 --> 00:02:28.655
it will already be in the cache.

00:02:28.655 --> 00:02:32.810
If you try to&nbsp;access memory that was already in the cache, we call that a cache hit.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:37.223
And if the thing that you're&nbsp;looking for isn't in the cache, then it's a cache miss.

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:42.023
So, misses are very expensive. Hits are not&nbsp;expensive.

00:02:42.023 --> 00:02:45.048
Here, I have a function for summing up elements of a vector.

00:02:45.048 --> 00:02:47.177
I take in the indices that&nbsp;I want to sum

00:02:47.177 --> 00:02:53.630
And then I just loop over them, grabbing that element out of the&nbsp;vector and adding it to the total.

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:58.678
Addition is commutative. So, I'm going&nbsp;to get the same sum regardless of the order that I add up the indices in.

00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:02.560
However,&nbsp;the order matters a lot for cache locality.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.726
If I access element 0 and then 1, 2, 3, 4 and so on,

00:03:06.726 --> 00:03:09.600
that's really easy for the processor to predict.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.400
But if the indices were a random permutation&nbsp;of all the possible valid indices of the vector,

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.600
then the processor is going to have no way to&nbsp;predict what memory to load next. It's just random.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:20.941
And here's the results.

00:03:20.941 --> 00:03:24.029
The blue line shows the in&nbsp;order access of the vector.

00:03:24.029 --> 00:03:27.245
So, just going index 0, 1, 2 and so on.

00:03:27.245 --> 00:03:32.558
Whereas, the red line is for accessing&nbsp;things in a random order and adding them up.

00:03:32.558 --> 00:03:35.235
The x-axis here is how many bytes long the vector&nbsp;is.

00:03:35.235 --> 00:03:40.845
So, approximately four times the length of the vector because the elements are integers.

00:03:40.845 --> 00:03:43.407
I chose to use bytes for the units instead of length

00:03:43.407 --> 00:03:47.932
Because, from this picture you can&nbsp;practically read off how big my caches are.

00:03:47.932 --> 00:03:50.773
The biggest kink in this graph is right here around&nbsp;8 megabytes.

00:03:50.773 --> 00:03:54.428
And I have 8 megabytes of L3 cache.

00:03:54.428 --> 00:03:58.827
That means, to the left, the computer could&nbsp;potentially put the entire vector into cache.

00:03:58.827 --> 00:04:02.681
Now that doesn't actually happen because it&nbsp;wouldn't really know to do that ahead of time.

00:04:02.681 --> 00:04:06.925
And there's other things using memory on my&nbsp;computer, of course.

00:04:06.925 --> 00:04:10.332
But to the right of the line, the entire vector won't fit into cache.

00:04:10.332 --> 00:04:15.052
So,&nbsp;if the computer isn't able to predict what memory you're going to access next ahead of time,

00:04:15.052 --> 00:04:18.560
then&nbsp;you're going to pay that penalty of cash misses.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.836
On the other hand, for the in order access pattern,

00:04:20.836 --> 00:04:25.910
it didn't seem to make any difference at all when&nbsp;we crossed the 8 megabyte cash threshold.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:32.160
That's because this access pattern is so predictable the computer can just put the data&nbsp;that you need into cache before you ever need it.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.270
Okay, accessing a vector's elements in a random&nbsp;order is a pretty uncommon and silly thing to do.

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:39.325
How about a more realistic example?

00:04:39.325 --> 00:04:41.645
Matrix&nbsp;multiplication

00:04:41.645 --> 00:04:45.470
Here's a straightforward way to implement matrix multiplication.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:49.122
I take&nbsp;a and b which represent N by N matrices.

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:53.536
So, they're both vectors that contain N times N&nbsp;elements.

00:04:53.536 --> 00:04:59.441
The purpose of the function is to put the matrix product of a and b into the output vector.

00:04:59.441 --> 00:05:02.960
We start by initializing the output to all zeros.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:07.135
Then we just do a simple double&nbsp;for-loop over the rows and columns.&nbsp;

00:05:07.135 --> 00:05:15.040
If you remember the ijth entry of the output&nbsp;matrix should be the sum over k of a_ik times b_kj&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.362
This third for-loop is to implement the sum.

00:05:18.362 --> 00:05:22.565
As we loop over k, i and j are fixed. So, this term isn't changing at all.

00:05:22.565 --> 00:05:24.778
This next term&nbsp;we are changing.

00:05:24.778 --> 00:05:28.443
But it's just moving with k, one element at a time.

00:05:28.443 --> 00:05:30.865
The problem is with this&nbsp;third term.

00:05:30.865 --> 00:05:37.415
Here, when k increases by 1 because we have N times k, we're now jumping N elements ahead.

00:05:37.415 --> 00:05:40.335
So, this is bad for cache locality.

00:05:40.335 --> 00:05:42.118
Here I have the exact same function,

00:05:42.118 --> 00:05:48.344
except the only difference is&nbsp;that instead of looping i, j, k, I'm looping i, k, j.

00:05:48.344 --> 00:05:53.040
Now let's take a look at the inner loop&nbsp;now that it's a loop over j instead of k.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:58.346
As j changes, this element is moving one element&nbsp;at a time in order. That's fine.

00:05:58.346 --> 00:06:02.217
i and k are fixed. So, this isn't changing at all. That's also fine.

00:06:02.217 --> 00:06:04.311
And now the third term is also okay.

00:06:04.311 --> 00:06:08.267
Again as j changes, this is just moving one element at a time.

00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:12.053
So, all three terms are good for cache locality.

00:06:12.053 --> 00:06:15.725
So, I went ahead and wrote a variation for&nbsp;all six orders of i, j and k.

00:06:15.725 --> 00:06:17.467
Let's compare the performance.

00:06:17.467 --> 00:06:22.610
And here, we have it a huge&nbsp;difference in performance just based off of the loop order.

00:06:22.610 --> 00:06:25.602
As we reasoned before, loops&nbsp;where the inner loop was j.

00:06:25.602 --> 00:06:30.676
So, i, k, j and k, i, j, those performed way better than the others.

00:06:30.676 --> 00:06:35.662
So, in&nbsp;this case, having good cache locality gave us over a 2x performance improvement.

00:06:35.662 --> 00:06:37.329
That's pretty&nbsp;good.

00:06:37.329 --> 00:06:40.446
These times were for 64 by 64 matrices.

00:06:40.446 --> 00:06:42.647
And when the matrices are that small,

00:06:42.647 --> 00:06:45.731
cache&nbsp;locality is pretty much the dominating feature.

00:06:45.731 --> 00:06:50.040
However, this is not the end of the line for&nbsp;bigger matrices.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:56.226
If you're working with bigger matrices, of course, doing block matrix algorithms&nbsp;across multiple threads is going to be faster.

00:06:56.226 --> 00:07:03.311
However, for smaller matrices, you can probably&nbsp;compute the entire answer in less time that it would take to spawn a single thread.

00:07:03.311 --> 00:07:09.607
The next&nbsp;factor that you should really consider when thinking about the performance of your code is&nbsp;branch predictability.

00:07:09.607 --> 00:07:13.206
A branch is just something that affects the control flow of your code,

00:07:13.206 --> 00:07:16.720
like,&nbsp;an if-statement or a for-loop or a while-loop.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:23.441
Whether or not the if statement turns out true&nbsp;or false determines what the next instruction for&nbsp;the program is going to be.

00:07:23.441 --> 00:07:26.430
Modern processors are&nbsp;really amazing in what they can do.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:30.737
Gone are the days where your program is executed line by line.

00:07:30.737 --> 00:07:35.309
A&nbsp;modern processor will happily execute things in a different order than you asked for.

00:07:35.309 --> 00:07:39.440
And potentially,&nbsp;even executing multiple things at the same time.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:44.249
If you want to add say 3 to some variable x and&nbsp;then 5 to another variable y,

00:07:44.249 --> 00:07:50.837
there's no reason the processor should wait in order for the store&nbsp;to x to be finished before starting the store to&nbsp;y.

00:07:50.837 --> 00:07:53.048
They can execute at the same time.

00:07:53.048 --> 00:07:57.184
There are many&nbsp;many situations like this that your processor will try to take advantage of.

00:07:57.184 --> 00:08:01.592
As long as you couldn't&nbsp;possibly tell the difference that anything funny was going on,

00:08:01.592 --> 00:08:05.809
your processor is free to do them&nbsp;out of order or in parallel.

00:08:05.809 --> 00:08:08.081
But what happens when you get to a branch point?

00:08:08.081 --> 00:08:13.901
Your processor&nbsp;can't know which instruction to execute next until it's fully evaluated the condition.

00:08:13.901 --> 00:08:17.471
In situations like this, your processor may just guess.

00:08:17.471 --> 00:08:23.218
If the last 10 loops your processor&nbsp;found that x was bigger than threshold and this one was executed,

00:08:23.218 --> 00:08:27.040
then on the 11th time, it&nbsp;might just start executing this ahead of time.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:31.280
As long as it predicted correctly, it gets&nbsp;a big head start on the next computation.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:34.639
We say that a branch is predictable or has&nbsp;good branch predictability

00:08:34.639 --> 00:08:37.225
if the processor is able to correctly predict

00:08:37.225 --> 00:08:40.812
which of the&nbsp;outcomes is going to occur a lot of the time.

00:08:40.812 --> 00:08:44.116
For this function get_product, I'm doing something&nbsp;kind of artificial.

00:08:44.116 --> 00:08:48.400
But it will allow us to really see how good the branch predictor is.

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.709
I start with&nbsp;a product of 1.0 and then I loop through all the elements.

00:08:51.709 --> 00:08:56.033
For each element that's bigger than&nbsp;some threshold, I multiply by 2 times the element.

00:08:56.033 --> 00:09:01.520
And if it's not bigger than the threshold,&nbsp;then I multiply by 1.5 times the element.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:04.720
In this case, the predictability of the branch&nbsp;depends a lot on

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:09.179
what percentage of elements&nbsp;in the vector are bigger than the threshold.

00:09:09.179 --> 00:09:11.400
If&nbsp;90% of things are bigger than the threshold,

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:15.973
then the branch predictor could just&nbsp;predict true and be right 90% of the time.

00:09:15.973 --> 00:09:18.365
If only 10% of things were bigger&nbsp;than the threshold,

00:09:18.365 --> 00:09:23.629
the branch predictor could just predict false all the time and still be right&nbsp;90% of the time.

00:09:23.629 --> 00:09:28.975
But if half are bigger and half are smaller, then the branch predictor can't do any&nbsp;better than 50%.

00:09:28.975 --> 00:09:31.183
And here's the graph that proves it.

00:09:31.183 --> 00:09:35.686
Here, I used a uniform random number&nbsp;generator to generate numbers between 0 and 1.

00:09:35.686 --> 00:09:39.099
and then set a threshold between 0 and 1.

00:09:39.099 --> 00:09:45.107
In&nbsp;both cases, when the threshold was either very low or very high, the branch became predictable.

00:09:45.107 --> 00:09:49.790
And we&nbsp;got a nearly 2x performance improvement from the branch predictor.

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:56.346
Of course, the worst performance&nbsp;is dead center at 50% when the branch predictor can do little more than flip a coin.

00:09:56.346 --> 00:09:57.482
I do want to warn&nbsp;you, though.

00:09:57.482 --> 00:09:59.068
Compilers are really really good.

00:09:59.068 --> 00:10:01.458
So, if you try to do this benchmark yourself,

00:10:01.458 --> 00:10:06.318
depending&nbsp;on the compiler and optimization settings and the time of day or whatever,

00:10:06.318 --> 00:10:08.270
you might get different&nbsp;results.

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:10.681
There's a good chance that if you try this with your compiler,

00:10:10.681 --> 00:10:13.417
you might see a completely flat&nbsp;line.

00:10:13.417 --> 00:10:14.877
And if that's the case,

00:10:14.877 --> 00:10:19.782
what probably happened is that your compiler completely optimized the&nbsp;branch away.

00:10:19.782 --> 00:10:23.013
There's a good chance your compiler would optimize it to something like this

00:10:23.013 --> 00:10:24.641
that&nbsp;doesn't involve any branching.

00:10:24.641 --> 00:10:27.768
And if that's the case, you would just see a flat line.

00:10:27.768 --> 00:10:29.620
Well, that's&nbsp;all I've got. Thanks for watching.

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:32.665
If you liked the video, don't forget to like comment and subscribe.

00:10:32.665 --> 00:10:42.466
Thanks to my patrons and donors for supporting me.

